h('How to use this handbook')
p('Read pages 3-7 to understand and run the app. Use pages 8-16 for content management. Pages 17-22 explain implementation, security, testing and deployment. The final section gives a staged MySQL-to-PostgreSQL migration plan.')
p('<b>Important:</b> this PDF documents the current source code and the development work discussed in this project. It is not an independent security certification. It does not contain real credentials, private applications, or contact messages. PostgreSQL migration is guidance only; the running app has not been switched.')

page('Contents & reading map')
table([['Pages','Topic'],['3-4','What was built; architecture and request flows'],['5-7','Source code map; local setup; seed and everyday commands'],['8-10','Public navigation, homepage carousel, jobs and applications'],['11-14','Blog, About, team, legal documents, gallery, Facebook and contact'],['15-16','Testimonials and the featured opportunity card'],['17-19','Authentication, upload handling and database model reference'],['20-22','Testing, troubleshooting, deployment and maintenance'],['23','References and verification boundaries'],['24-29','PostgreSQL: preparation, schema conversion, data transfer, sequence repair, cutover and rollback']])
h('Terminology')
p('<b>Public dashboard:</b> the visitor-facing homepage, not the administrator console. <b>CRUD:</b> create, read, update and delete. <b>Published:</b> visible to visitors. <b>Draft:</b> saved in the database but normally hidden from public pages. <b>Prisma Client:</b> generated TypeScript/JavaScript code used to query your database.')
p('<b>Schema:</b> the model definitions in prisma/schema.prisma. <b>Seed:</b> a setup script that inserts initial records. <b>Migration:</b> a versioned database structure change; moving existing data between different database engines also needs a separate transfer process.')
h('Commands and paths')
p('Commands are intended to run from the project root. File names throughout this guide are relative to that root unless stated otherwise. Examples use placeholders instead of passwords. Do not copy placeholder values unchanged into production.')
code('D:\\PROJECT  FOLDER\\e-commerce\\di recruitment webpage')

page('01 / What was built')
p('The project evolved from a visually styled recruitment homepage into a database-backed content management application. The public website presents recruitment information; the authenticated admin area manages content and reviews visitor submissions.')
step(1,'Foundation','A Next.js App Router project uses React components, TypeScript types, CSS styling, Prisma models and a MySQL connection. Server-rendered pages read published records from the database.')
step(2,'Recruitment workflows','Job management and application submission were connected to MySQL. The homepage now previews at most four jobs. A separate /jobs page lists all published jobs and supports client-side search, location filters and applications.')
step(3,'Content management','Blog posts, ordered team members, About pages, leadership portraits and legal document images became editable in admin. Public pages read saved records rather than requiring code edits for each update.')
step(4,'Media and engagement','Event folders support photos/videos. A Facebook Page embed appears on both public and admin dashboards. Homepage slides and testimonials rotate every five seconds when multiple published items exist.')
step(5,'Owner controls','Contact enquiries are collected on a separate page. Admin can review/delete them. A dedicated featured opportunity editor controls the hero card. Password changes and strict upload limits were added.')
step(6,'Refinements','The public navbar stays at the top during scrolling. Draft/published carousel status is now explicit. A stale Prisma Client that lacked newly added models was regenerated, and the server was restarted.')
h('What remains fixed in code')
p('Not every word is CMS-managed. Some homepage introductory text, service copy, statistics, contact information and styling remain in app/page.tsx and CSS. Verify all public business claims before deployment. There is no full drag-and-drop page builder.')

page('02 / Architecture & data flow')
table([['Layer','Responsibility'],['Browser / React','Renders cards and forms; filters jobs; previews files; runs carousel timers.'],['Next.js server','Renders database-backed pages, processes server actions and API requests.'],['Validation / authorization','Zod checks inputs. requireAdmin or session checks protect administrative writes.'],['Prisma Client','Provides typed database access, generated from the schema.'],['MySQL','Stores content, users, settings, file references and submissions.'],['Persistent disk','Stores uploaded media under storage/gallery, storage/team and storage/content.']])
h('Admin content update, step by step')
p('1. Admin signs in and opens an editor. 2. The browser submits FormData to a server action. 3. The server verifies the session and validates fields. 4. It validates and stores any new image. 5. Prisma inserts or updates a record. 6. The action revalidates affected routes. 7. A fresh public page request reads the updated published data.')
h('Visitor submission, step by step')
p('The job application modal posts JSON to /api/applications. The server validates and saves an Application linked to a Job. The contact page instead calls a server action and saves a ContactSubmission. Both workflows remain separate so a general enquiry does not need a job ID.')
h('Why files are not all in public/')
p('Uploaded draft images must not be publicly accessible just because their URL is known. Dedicated media routes look up database references and publication state before serving stored files. Static design assets can remain in public/images.')

page('03 / Source code map')
